Local runtime corrections and host integration.
Drop each part when a released version carries it, and the whole patch (and
the patchedDependencies entry in package.json) when all are released and Ave
pins that version.
Transport failures use 502/504 and attributed failure records; policy refusals
remain 403. DNS, route lookup, connect, and timeout errors stay fail-closed.
test/scripts/asrt-patched-symbols.test.ts fails if any part is missing from
the installed package.

1. https://github.com/anthropics/sandbox-runtime/pull/722 (issue #721): a
   per-wrap `ttys` option that grants file-ioctl on the named pty slave only,
   so the workspace terminal's shell can set its own tty (no double echo,
   working job control) without allowPty's grant on every /dev/ttys*.

2. https://github.com/anthropics/sandbox-runtime/pull/735 (issue #734): the
   ask callback is told which invocation asked. `SandboxAskCallback` receives
   a second argument `{ attributionKey }`, the decoded proxy-username key (the
   wrap's `commandId`) that the per-command lists and the violation store are
   keyed by. Without it an embedder that runs several invocations through the
   one process-wide proxy cannot tell which of them opened the connection it
   is asked about. Carried regardless of upstream review (owner 2026-10-19).

3. Invocation listeners (Ave, upstream proposal drafted in the PR that added
   it; related to sandbox-runtime#160): `SandboxManager.openInvocationListener`
   gives one invocation its own proxy front end (a loopback port on macOS that
   the wrap's Seatbelt profile alone allows, a socket path on Linux bound into
   that bwrap), attributes its connections from the listener rather than the
   forgeable proxy username (`SandboxAskContext.identity`), refuses requests
   for any proxy listener's own port on loopback or this host's own
   addresses (so one invocation cannot hop onto another's listener through
   the proxy), and bounds the count. An ended listener's macOS port stays
   bound for a quarantine period (30 minutes), longer while the invocation's
   process group (`closeInvocationListener(id, { processGroup })`) still has
   a process in it, up to 24 hours; then it is released, its number is never
   handed out again and is still refused as a hop target. On Linux
   every lane socket and every mux HTTP backend socket lives in one private
   0700 directory, and every Linux wrap mounts a tmpfs over that directory and
   binds back only its own lane socket, so no wrap can reach another
   invocation's socket even when allowAllUnixSockets lifts the AF_UNIX block.
   A destination name is resolved once, after the ask callback allowed the
   name (part 5) and before its resolved phase, and refused when any address
   is one the resolved-address guard refuses; the resolved phase is told the
   addresses (`SandboxAskResolvedContext.addresses`) and the connection is pinned to them: a
   direct dial uses them instead of resolving again, and a parent-proxy
   CONNECT names an address literal instead of the name, trying the vetted
   addresses in turn (IPv4 first) until the upstream opens one (SNI inside
   the tunnel is the client's). So neither DNS rebinding between the check and
   the dial nor the upstream's own resolution reaches an address the guard
   refuses. A plain-HTTP request by name through a parent proxy cannot be
   pinned (its absolute-form target names the host, which the upstream
   resolves), so it is treated like a name this host cannot resolve. A name
   this host cannot resolve is refused without a parent
   proxy; through a remote one it is forwarded by name and the callback is
   told `resolution: 'upstream'` (unverified); through one on this host
   (loopback, localhost-style names, an interface address) it is refused
   unless `parentProxy.resolvesNames` trusts it to resolve names. When a name
   is forwarded, the proxy socket actually connected is judged as well (its
   remoteAddress must not be this host) before anything is written to it,
   since the dial resolves the proxy's name again after that check. Test seams `setDestinationResolverForTesting` and
   `setListenerQuarantineForTesting` are named exports of
   dist/sandbox/sandbox-manager.js only (not on SandboxManager, not from the
   package entry) and throw outside the test runner (VITEST unset). `closeInvocationConnections(commandIds, host,
   port)` closes the open client connections those invocations made to a
   destination (the embedder calls it when it revokes a grant). The macOS
   profile also
   denies process-info on other processes and the kern.procargs sysctls
   (defense in depth for #160, which the embedder pairs with a new session per
   wrap). `openHostInvocationProxy` (a host-only named export from the manager
   module) supplies a private authenticated relay for externally confined
   Windows invocations. Its port and the registered host frontend ports join
   the same destination and upstream guards; both binds exclude the configured
   shared proxy range. Its listener identity, refusal records and tracked
   connections use the same policy and revocation paths as invocation listeners.

4. Upstream routes (Ave): `SandboxManager.setParentProxyRouter(router)`
   replaces the single static `parentProxy` with a per-destination hook. The
   filter asks `router({ host, port, scheme })` once per decision, after the
   name phase allowed it (part 5) and before the resolved phase (canonical
   host; scheme 'https' for CONNECT/SOCKS, 'http' for
   plain HTTP), and judges every answered route (`direct`, or an
   `http`/`https` proxy with optional Basic `auth` and `resolvesNames`) the
   way part 3 judges the one parent proxy: a pinned name or literal goes
   direct to its vetted addresses or CONNECTs to them as literals; a
   plain-HTTP name keeps its direct routes pinned and goes by name over the
   proxy routes that may carry a name (`resolution: 'upstream'`); a name this host cannot resolve
   drops direct routes and untrusted proxies on this host and is forwarded by
   name over the rest (`resolution: 'upstream'`); a route naming one of the
   session's proxy listeners is dropped, and the socket every proxy route
   actually reaches is judged against those listeners too. The allowed
   connection is dialed over the kept routes in order: only a route that
   fails to connect (refused, reset, timed out, proxy name unresolvable)
   hands over to the next; a policy refusal on a route (its socket reached
   this host or a listener) ends it, so a recorded refusal is the answer the
   client gets; a 407 refuses with "upstream proxy requires authentication"
   (CONNECT status line or plain-HTTP response, never relayed to the
   client); 502/504 CONNECT answers remain transport failures, and other
   non-2xx answers refuse with "upstream proxy refused the connection
   (HTTP <status>)", so neither a later proxy nor
   DIRECT carries what an upstream refused. Within one route, only a
   connection failure tries the next vetted destination address; every HTTP
   response is final. A loopback destination is always direct, a
   router that throws or answers a malformed route refuses, and an empty
   answer refuses with the router's reason. Without a router the static
   `parentProxy` is turned into the same routes, so behaviour is unchanged.
   `closeAllInvocationConnections()` closes every open
   connection the proxy allowed (the embedder calls it when the upstream
   setting changes). The router may expose synchronous `generation()` and
   `decisionClass(host, addresses, resolution)` hooks. A decision captures the
   generation; changes while awaiting approval, routing or DNS re-plan under
   the new configuration before dialing or writing credentials. Name-phase
   allowances remain held, resolved allowances are reused only for the same
   policy class, and denials stay final. Each socket attempt, proxy DNS answer
   and connected peer is checked again; plain HTTP also checks immediately
   before creating its request. Repeated changes fail closed. reset() clears
   the router.

5. Name-first decisions (Ave): the ask callback is asked in two phases.
   The name phase (`SandboxAskContext.phase: 'name'`, no addresses) runs
   right after the configured and per-command lists, before anything is
   resolved or routed; a denial there ends the connection with no DNS query
   and no router call, so a destination that will be refused cannot carry
   data out through a name lookup (the proxy's lookup is the sandbox's only
   resolver). Only a name-phase `true` leads to the routes and the
   resolution, and then to the resolved phase (`phase: 'resolved'` with
   `addresses` and `resolution`), whose answer decides the connection. A
   destination a configured or per-command list allows goes straight to the
   routes and the resolution, as before.

6. External confinement (Ave): `windows.externalConfinement: "embedder"`
   runs only the proxy on Windows when the embedder confines its children.
   Dependency checks and initialization skip the account, network fence and
   session ACL setup; wrapWithSandboxArgv() refuses an unprovisioned wrap.

7. Owned mount points (Ave): absent deny-path mount points are created
   during planning. Cleanup selects entries matching a recorded owned
   identity, subject to the pathname-syscall limits below. A file is opened
   with 'wx' and its bigint device/inode read with fstat. A
   directory is made under a private random name beside the target, its
   identity read with fstat on an O_DIRECTORY|O_NOFOLLOW descriptor, then
   renamed onto a reservation made at the target with an exclusive mkdir
   (an entry anyone else creates there first wins with EEXIST and is not
   claimed; a reservation replaced by a non-empty directory or non-directory
   fails the wrap closed). The measured descriptors stay open until their
   ownership records retire, preventing inode-number reuse from aliasing a
   replacement. Concurrent cleanup deferral and cached-source retries retain
   them. Ordinary planning failures use batch cleanup, including deferral;
   staged publication failures close their descriptor. If force runs during
   async planning, records created afterward wait for a later cleanup or
   process exit, even if that planning call then fails.
   Cleanup, and removal of the private empty mount
   source, move a matching entry to a private random name, check the
   identity and emptiness again there, and delete only that name. An entry
   found not to be ours (or ours but not empty) is put back without
   overwriting (link() for a non-directory, otherwise only while the name is
   absent); if the name is taken it stays at the private name and is logged.
   Residual limit: Node has no renameat2(RENAME_NOREPLACE/EXCHANGE),
   funlinkat or openat, so each step is still a path syscall. An entry can
   still be lost only in these windows between two consecutive syscalls:
   any rename that puts an empty directory in place of the reservation
   (renaming ours away and creating one, or renaming an existing empty
   directory over it) between the reservation mkdir and the publishing
   rename replaces that empty directory (a non-empty directory or a
   non-directory there fails the wrap closed); a swap of one of the 128-bit
   private names (staged directory before its descriptor is opened,
   quarantine before it is checked or deleted); or, after a replacement was
   already moved aside, an entry created at the public name between the
   absence check and the rename that puts a directory back (an empty
   directory there is replaced; a non-directory likewise where hard links
   are refused). During cleanup, an accidental concurrent create, replace
   or atomic save at the target can only skip the cleanup; an entry moved
   aside and put back is briefly absent from its name.
   Incompatible bind targets still fail closed. Concurrent-wrap cleanup
   remains deferred until all wraps have ended.
   If publication fails after reserving the target, its reservation may
   remain unclaimed: that identity was not recorded. Debug logging reports
   the target for inspection instead of risking removal of a replacement.

8. Caller-owned writes: default write paths contain only device paths. All
   filesystem write roots come from caller policy.

9. Unix socket denial (Ave): `WrapWithSandboxOptions.denyUnixSockets` refuses
   Unix sockets for one invocation, overriding shared worker socket grants
   for shell commands.

10. Linux mandatory Git controls (Ave) are mounted read-only only when they
    exist. Missing controls stay absent during preparation; the host
    quarantines newly created metadata after execution. Other mandatory
    missing-path denies remain.

11. Folded path filters (Ave): every macOS rule renders the `subpath` and
    `literal` filters that share a parent directory as one anchored regex
    per parent (`^<dir>/(<a>|<b>)(/.*)?$`, or `...)$` for literals), chunked
    under the SBPL string limit. The filters of a rule are alternatives, so
    the regex matches exactly the paths they matched. sandbox-exec compiles
    every wrap's profile again, and path filters cost more than their
    number (a worker profile of about 500 compiled in ~0.1 s locally and
    ~0.7 s on a loaded hosted runner); folded, about 75 remain.
    `setPathFilterCompactionForTesting` renders them unfolded for tests.

12. Host-owned release (Ave): reset() judges the session's Windows ACL
    release by srt-win's own outcome. revokeWindowsAcl/restoreWindowsAcl
    take an `onExit(ok)` callback, because srt-win exits non-zero and leaves
    a path out of its JSON when release_one_ace fails for it
    (vendor/srt-win-src/src/cli.rs:1292-1320 at v0.0.80, "N FAILED (ACE
    left in place)"). Statuses follow AceRelease
    (vendor/srt-win-src/src/state_db.rs:1066-1097): revoked, stillHeld,
    downgraded, relocated and noRow are completed releases, as are the
    legacy restored/alreadyOriginal. mismatch and missing already deleted
    the holder row, so a retry changes nothing; a mismatched object's ACE is
    left as it was (a deny stays fail-closed). Recovery cannot revisit
    rows already dropped. reset() resolves with those counted per step in
    `SandboxResetReport.windowsAclUnreleased` (in-process targets for host redaction). srt-win not
    running (undefined), a non-zero exit or any other status is a failure:
    reset() finishes the rest of the teardown, then rejects with
    `WindowsSandboxError` code `acl_release_failed`, naming only step and
    status, and keeps the session's ACL state so a later reset() (the
    'exit' handler's) tries again. Upstream logged all of this only under
    SRT_DEBUG and resolved.
    `SandboxManager.setSignalCleanupOwnedByHost(true)`, called before the
    first initialize(), stops registerCleanup() from adding its SIGINT and
    SIGTERM reset, for an embedder whose own signal handling drains its
    children first and then calls reset(); the 'exit' handler is kept.
    `setWindowsAclReleaseForTesting` (manager module only, test runner only)
    gives reset() an ACL session with stand-ins for srt-win's revoke/restore.

13. Recovery report (Ave): srt-win's crash recovery (dead holders pruned,
    orphan sandbox-user ACEs revoked) was reported only in the stderr of the
    first acl command, which reaches the embedder only under SRT_DEBUG.
    initialize() now runs `srt-win acl recover --json` on its own before the
    session's first grant (its `{deadBrokers, acesRevoked}` output,
    vendor/srt-win-src/src/cli.rs AclCmd::Recover at v0.0.80) and hands the
    counts to the listener set with `setWindowsAclRecoveryListener` (a
    named export of dist/sandbox/windows-sandbox-utils.js). A failed recover
    fails initialize, as a failed grant or stamp does.

14. Asynchronous session isolation. `SandboxManager.initializeWindowsIsolation`
    prepares a proxy-only session's account, egress fence and ACLs without
    blocking the event loop. The existing 60-second ACL ceilings apply to
    recovery, grant, stamp and partial-failure release. Until every step
    succeeds, the external-confinement wrap refusal remains active. Promotion
    preserves current proxy traffic and connection policy. Failure leaves the
    proxy running, and reset invalidates promotion before awaiting and releasing
    any partial ACL state. Concurrent resets share one completion and release.
    Ordinary initialize uses the same preparation path.

15. Object-local parent deletion guards. The carried native source patch keeps
    the parent guard on its own object, adds inheritable delete-child protection
    to full denies, and preserves propagation for inheritable policy changes
    and legacy cleanup. Native builds verify fixed source and patch inputs,
    then embed their exact binary receipts in the host bundle.

16. Durable deny ownership. Base lists reconcile by owner, principal and target
    identity; unchanged completed sets attach live holds without ACL writes.
    Delta and teardown intent is durable before effects, while active holders
    retain their obligations. Recovery repairs only actual interrupted tree
    writes. Explicit disable and uninstall remove durable ownership; ordinary
    reset and exit release only live holds. Principal replacement cannot
    repurpose the owner's or another account's access entries.

17. Stamp diagnostics. Debug output records each input target, its absolute
    parent and deny kind before native work, followed by elapsed stamp time.
    It contains paths, never file contents or credential values.
